What is a 2D Array?
    A 2D array is an array of arrays. It stores multiple values of the same data type in a
    structure that looks like a table or matrix.

    Important points:
         A 2D array stores data in rows and columns.
         It is an array whose elements are themselves arrays.
         Indexing starts from 0.
         Each element is accessed using two indexes: row and column.

2D Array Declaration and Initialization
    A. Declaration
        Syntax:    dataType[][] arrayName;
        example -> int[][] numbers;
                   double[][] prices;
                   String[][] names;
                   int numbers[][];

    B. Declaration and initialization together
        int[][] numbers = {
            {10, 20, 30},
            {40, 50, 60},
            {70, 80, 90}
        };

Creating a 2D array using "new"
    Syntax:     dataType[][] arrayName = new dataType[rows][columns];
    Example:    int[][] numbers = new int[3][3];

Declaration and initialization separately
    int[][] numbers;

    numbers = new int[][] {
        {10, 20},
        {30, 40}
    };

Nested Loops for Array Traversal
    In a 2D array, we use nested loops because there are two dimensions.
    - Outer loop → traverses rows.
    - Inner loop → traverses columns.

        public static void main(String[] args) {

                int[][] numbers = {
                    {10, 20, 30},
                    {40, 50, 60},
                    {70, 80, 90}
                };

                for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {

                    for (int j = 0; j < numbers[i].length; j++) {

                        System.out.print(numbers[i][j] + " ");
                    }

                    System.out.println();
                }
        }
Accessing and Updating Matrix Elements
    To access an element, use:      arrayName[rowIndex][columnIndex]
    Example -
            int[][] numbers = {
                {10, 20, 30},
                {40, 50, 60},
                {70, 80, 90}
            };

            // Accessing
            System.out.println(numbers[0][1]);

            // Updating
            numbers[0][1] = 100;

            System.out.println(numbers[0][1]);

Calculate the sum of all elements
    public static void main(String[] args) {

            int[][] numbers = {
                {10, 20, 30},
                {40, 50, 60},
                {70, 80, 90}
            };

            int sum = 0;

            for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {

                for (int j = 0; j < numbers[i].length; j++) {

                    sum += numbers[i][j];
                }
            }

            System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
        }

Taking 2D Array Input Using Scanner

        public static void main(String[] args) {

            Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

            System.out.print("Enter rows: ");
            int rows = sc.nextInt();

            System.out.print("Enter columns: ");
            int columns = sc.nextInt();

            int[][] matrix = new int[rows][columns];

            // Taking input
            for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {

                for (int j = 0; j < columns; j++) {

                    System.out.print(
                        "Enter element [" + i + "][" + j + "]: "
                    );

                    matrix[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
                }
            }

            // Displaying matrix
            System.out.println("Your Matrix:");

            for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {

                for (int j = 0; j < columns; j++) {

                    System.out.print(matrix[i][j] + "\t");
                }

                System.out.println();
            }

            sc.close();
        }
Jagged Arrays – Basic Introduction
    A jagged array is a 2D array in which each row can have a different number of columns.
    In a regular 2D array, all rows have the same number of columns.
    In a jagged array, row lengths can differ.

    Example -
                int[][] numbers = new int[3][];

                numbers[0] = new int[2];
                numbers[1] = new int[3];
                numbers[2] = new int[4];

Difference Between 1D and 2D Arrays
  1.  Feature	            1D Array	    2D Array
  2.  Structure	            Linear	        Tabular
  3.  Dimensions          	One	            Two
  4.  Indexes	            One	            Two
  5.  Declaration	        int[] a	        int[][] a
  6.  Access	            a[0]	        a[0][0]
  7.  Traversal         	One loop    	Nested loops